{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power","id":1561998,"title":"Nightmare 4 \u2013 Waking Nightmare \u2013 How do you handle the power?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-07-21T09:33:00-07:00","id":1561998,"title":"Nightmare 4 \u2013 Waking Nightmare \u2013 How do you handle the power?","content":"Hello all,<br /> <br /> While I love nightmares and the roleplaying possibility of its 4th dot of nightmares, as an ST, I always struggle a bit adjudicating the limit of such a broadly written power.<br /> <br /> On one end, I want to encourage player imagination and respect the fact that it\u2019s a fourth dot of a discipline : It should be strong.<br /> <br /> Multiple other discipline powers can end conflict in a single test and essentially make their victim at the character\u2019s mercy ; Mesmerize, Confidant, Face of the Beast, The Grand Delusion, and multiple other devotions.<br /> <br /> On the other end, even strong powers, such as Loyalty have clear limits to what they can achieve.<br /> <br /> Here are some examples of the powerful effects I have allowed to go through as an ST : <hr /><b>1st example \u2013 massive earthquake </b><br /> <br /> <b>Scene : </b>A street brawl between the coterie and some dangerous revenants.<br /> <br /> <b>Player Intention :</b> Remove an opponent from battle.<br /> <br /> <b>Illusion :</b> A massive earthquake that makes city buildings crumble, causing dangerous falling debris everywhere.<br /> <br /> <b>Result : </b>The target panicked, succeeded in a few athletic tests to dodge falling debris to avoid falling prone, and fled the scene to somewhere safer, looking extremely confused and somewhat relieved when they realized they had been on a massive psychedelic trip this whole time. <hr /><b>2nd example \u2013 Replicating Clones </b><br /> <br /> <b>Scene : </b>The gangrel player character had a dramatic failure on a frenzy test after being insulted by their snarky nosferatu teammate, who didn\u2019t realize the gangrel was this close to blowing up.<br /> <br /> <b>Player Intention :</b> Distract the raging beast to avoid being ripped to shreds by their coterie mate.<br /> <br /> <b>Illusion : </b>Replicating taunting clones of the user to distract the gangrel long enough from them to flee the scene.<br /> <br /> <b>Result :</b> A very messy shared haven, but a safe nosferatu and a calmed down gangrel.<br /> <br /> <b>Frenzy Justification : </b>I allowed it because mental discipline can redirect the beast, not its want. Since all senses were affected, I judged that illusions could redirect the beast\u2019s anger to distract the gangrel. <hr /><b>Questions </b><br /> <br /> When does its victim start to realize that they might be trapped in an illusion and what can they do about it?<br /> <br /> Is there anyway to break off the effect?<ul><li>I would personally think that Auspex might trigger Clash of Will. I\u2019m tempted to say that increased sense from Protean might as well.</li> </ul><br /> How do you handle the power yourself?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-21T18:56:42-07:00","id":1562021,"title":"","content":"Precedent from Obfuscate indicates that Discipline-based illusions are durable enough that even if you suspect your perceptions are being meddled with there's not much you can do to fix your senses by default. A Devotion akin to Shatter the Shroud might be used to proactively break through Waking Nightmare, but the standard kit doesn't really have anything for this.<br /> <br /> Waking Nightmare affects all the senses, and while a case could be made for allowing Auspex to discern the illusion like it can with Obfuscate, I don't think sensory boosts from Protean help any more than glasses or hearing aids would \u2014 physically altering your body, even through magical means, isn't going to do anything to help with an attack on your psyche.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-21T22:28:31-07:00","id":1562024,"title":"","content":"I'm with Satchel.<br /> <br /> This kind of illusion don't rely on being believed: it hijacks the senses directly.<br /> <br /> You can have a character logically conclude the effect is not right, but that won't stop their every sense from screaming it's real.<br /> <br /> From your examples: the Revenant could know this is most likely an illusion from the known illusionist in the coterie, but the ground shaking and buildings crumbling are no less real to the senses and seeking cover isn't just avoiding debris they can't say for sure to not be real, it's also avoiding real attacks they'll be incapable of distinguishing from the illusion.<br /> <br /> And if somehow the frenzied Gangrel concluded it was an illusion, they'd be still incapable of telling which one is real.<br /> <br /> So most of the time the question is actually moot: not believing the illusion won't change much, and any decision taken at this point is risky.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-22T01:07:52-07:00","id":1562034,"title":"","content":"The way I personally handle Walking Nightmare is that, if the power's roll succed, depending on the effect described by the user (which can be either a player or a NPC) the power affects the victim even though he might be aware it's just an hallucination, then he will get penalties to any roll equal to the successes rolled on the activation roll, until the nightmare is up.<br /> Another option could be to give malus equal to Nightmare level (either 4 or 5) both options are viable for me.<br /> <br /> Then, if the user activates Walking Dead to prevent the victim to go through a certain direction, e.g.: by making the doorway appearing as a kraken's Jaws, or on fire, or the alley seem severed by a crater, with hellish hands reaching out from its depths, then the victim must take a roll on Resolve+Composure- with the penalities discussed above to be able to just going through the hallucination.<br /> <br /> The difference between these two cases is that, in the first case, the victim is passively distracted and terrified by the environment. In the second case he has to take an effort to go through a specific hallucination. <br /> <br /> Another application I faced during some of my game is using Walking Nightmare to make an ally appearing as a monster (or the enemy they're facing) hoping for the victim to attack the ally in a isntictive reaction. In this case cannot force the player to do a specific action but in order to understand that it may be an hallucination he has to succes on the same reolve+composure roll-nightmare (or - successes rolled on the activation roll)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-22T10:59:59-07:00","id":1562051,"title":"","content":"Additional note that the most straightforward benefit of Waking Nightmare is supplementing or triggering the Conditions created by Face of the Beast and Grand Delusion \u2014 causing targets to act on a belief or fear that might not otherwise be relevant is incredibly easy when you can force a fake person, object, or location feature into their sensory experience.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-22T15:55:53-07:00","id":1562063,"title":"","content":"I really like Marcus' split between passive dread and having to actively push through a specific hallucination, since it gives you a clean way to scale the Resolve + Composure roll to the stakes. And I'm with Satchel and monteparnas that realizing it's fake shouldn't just switch it off. My rule of thumb: knowing it's an illusion lets a character act despite the fear, but it never removes the sensory input, so they still can't tell the real threat from the fake one. That way the power stays a fourth-dot heavy hitter without being an auto-win, and clever players are rewarded with agency rather than immunity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29129-daria22","name":"daria22","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2026"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-22T19:57:35-07:00","id":1562071,"title":"","content":"Precisely. Powerful illusions aren't useful because they deceive the target, they're useful because they sensory input is a thing in itself. It's intrusive, obscure things, confusing and even scary for the sake of being there.<br /> <br /> Just think about how we get invested in or nervous about things we know perfectly well to not be real. Scary movies, horror books, going to a Haunted House attraction. Schizophrenics nowadays go through this a lot: they know their hallucinations aren't real and many have good, working strategies to deal with them. But they are there nonetheless, no going around it.<br /> <br /> We're me in a simulation room of a whole city block crumbling to an earthquake I can feel for real, knowing it's fake wouldn't change much of the experience, and that's would still be a fairly controlled experience in my own terms, rather than a crazy fucker getting me by surprise and able to hide in the fake debris to stab me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-24T08:09:01-07:00","id":1562126,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Oh, thanks for your replies all!</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I tend to agree with all of you. As the power is written, it doesn\u2019t leave much room wiggle room to get out of it. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power?p=1562021#post1562021\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Waking Nightmare affects all the senses, and while a case could be made for allowing Auspex to discern the illusion like it can with Obfuscate, I don't think sensory boosts from Protean help any more than glasses or hearing aids would \u2014 physically altering your body, even through magical means, isn't going to do anything to help with an attack on your psyche.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">That\u2019s true. My idea was that creative use of protean to gain echolocation might allow a kindred to glimpse extra detail that the nightmare user didn\u2019t factor into when they used Waking Nightmare. It\u2019s definitely a stretch, though.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power?p=1562024#post1562024\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> From your examples: the Revenant could know this is most likely an illusion from the known illusionist in the coterie, but the ground shaking and buildings crumbling are no less real to the senses and seeking cover isn't just avoiding debris they can't say for sure to not be real, it's also avoiding real attacks they'll be incapable of distinguishing from the illusion.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Yeah, that\u2019s how I ended up running it; The revenant was definitely wondering how the hell it was happening, but they were still feeling rock impacts, their sense of balance being thrown off, etc. They were pretty much instantly taken out of the fight. Which is what nightmare excels at; that\u2019s fine. Especially since it was used to remove a nameless NPC from the scene. For players I\u2019ll probably allow a bit more leeway and allow a creative solution to at least give a possible out.</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As for Marcus, I really like your mechanical solutions, which what was I felt like the power was lacking. Since the power is already contested, I\u2019ll go with nightmares as a penalty.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Maybe I\u2019ll bake in something like Obfuscate 3 for less intense power, something like needing a successful Resolve + Wits test needing a number of successes equal to the activation, with a penalty equal to Nightmare, to find out that you are indeed in an illusion.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Using nightmares to make allies appear like enemies is wicked. I\u2019ll keep that in mind for my current Nosferatu PC when I eventually get Nigthmare at 4 dots, lol.</span><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-04T09:45:48-07:00","id":1562653,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Sorry for the double post, I want to use some examples of waking nightmare and ask you how you would handle each of them or if you would allow them at all.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I ask because I want to see where I stand compared to others, especially in relation to its limitations; one detail, no significant pain and its thematic limit.</span> <hr /><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>1. Abyssal Rift</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Scene : </b>A Nosferatu is ambushed by hunters in an alley street and uses waking nightmare in hope to reverse the role.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Illusion : </b>The ground breaks under everyone\u2019s feet, opening a wide bottomless rift black as night.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Question :</b> Which interpretation feels better? A, B, C, or none. If so, how would you handle it?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Interpretation A : </b>The Hunter feels the ground break hunter their feet. Each of them falls to the ground and the world around them slowly feels more distant as they ''fall'' further down the chasm. From the Nosferatu perspective, the hunters are on their backs clawing the air and panicking, leaving the Nosferatu ample opportunity to send them to early retirement.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Interpretation B :</b> Since the illusion is already affecting the ground, it can\u2019t affect other\u2019s \u2018\u2019location\u2019\u2019 relative to the place, nor can it obfuscate the Nosferatu. The hunters can see each other standing on a black rift; the hunters feel their whole sense of balance thrown off*.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Some may fall, some more dextrous than others barely manage to stay on their two feet. They can see the Nosferatu walking above the rift unhindered and they realize they have been messing with the wrong kindred. The battle isn\u2019t completely won for the Nosferatu, but the hunter shouldn\u2019t pose much problem.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">*Having done something similar for fun in a VR, walking on the edge of a building into \u2018\u2019nothing\u2019\u2019 it is truly dissorienting. It was fun to experience what an actual, albeit weak, illusion could be like in real life.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Interpretation C :</b> A mix of both A and B. Hunters are affected by the illusions, they, since they all act in accordance with the illusions, they all see themselves fall. However they can clearly see the nosferatu move unhindered in the pit. After a few seconds, some might realize that the walls of the nearby building aren't getting smaller as they fall. The effect is simillar to B.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Opinion :</b> I\u2019m a bit torn. I honestly don\u2019t know which one I\u2019d prefer. I would generally lean toward A if a player used the power on hunters, or on B if a player was the victim of the power to allow some tests to perceive some clues and allow an \u2018\u2019out\u2019\u2019.</span> <hr /><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>2. Solipsism</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Illusion : </b>The victim appears to be alone ; their senses can\u2019t pick up anyone in the vicinity, they feel the crushing weight of solitude.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Question :</b> Is this a valid use of the power?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Valid :</b> The power affects a single feature in the location. Having people in a location is a feature of the location, thus removing everyone is a feature in itself.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Invalid :</b> This affects too many features, the power can\u2019t change more than a single person, every person in the room is too many.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Opinion : </b>I would allow it, it\u2019s a powerful effect, it kind of overlaps with obfuscate, but both are illusory powers, one just happens to focus on fear more.</span> <hr /><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>3. Sun Rise</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Scene : </b>A Nosferatu is defending its territory from rival kindreds they caught feeding in an alleyway of their territory. They use waking nightmare to conjure the image of a rising sun to urge them to flee.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Illusion : </b>The Sun rises in the distance, its glaring rays begin to reach the kindred in the alleyway.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Question 1:</b> Can Nightmare conjure illusions of the sun? Or fire?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Opinion : </b>On one end, the Sun and fire are definitely among the scariest things for vampires. On the other, it feels a bit weird thematically for a vampire. I\u2019m torn.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Question 2 :</b> If yes, which interpretation sounds like the correct one?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Interpretation A : </b>The kindred affected by the illusion feel the burning gaze of the sun. They test against fear frenzy at -1 (if humanity 5 or above) or at -3 (if humanity 4 or lower). Those that fail run away, seeking the nearest available cover from the sun. Those that succeed realize that while the air feels hotter and the sun is uncomfortable, they don\u2019t actually feel significant pain so they ignore its effect. The most informed kindred realize that they are facing a Nosferatu that can conjure illusions; the less informed ones wonder why they are suddenly resistant to the sun. Let\u2019s hope, for their sakes, that they don't think they can resist the true sun.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Interpretation B : </b>The kindred affected by the illusion feel the burning gaze of the sun. They test against fear frenzy at -1 (if humanity 5 or above) or at -3 (if humanity 4 or lower). Those that fail run away, seeking the nearest available cover from the sun. Those that succeed might realize that there is an illusion, but the sun still feels real, and while it doesn\u2019t hurt <i>significantly </i>it\u2019s still hot and uncomfortable and they can feel their beast panicking as they urge them to flee for cover. They might stave off the frenzy for a bit longer, but they should not linger. They need to test for frenzy at each interval of fake \u2018\u2019sunlight damage\u2019\u2019 they would take.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Opinion : </b>I would go toward B. This is where i would probably end up using Marcus house rule for rolling composure + resolve \u2013 Nightmare to ignore the effect.</span> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Do you have any other interesting examples that come to mind that you allowed or your storyteller allowed you, or a fellow coterie mate, to use in a chronicle?</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-04T13:10:31-07:00","id":1562681,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power?p=1562653#post1562653\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Sorry for the double post, I want to use some examples of waking nightmare and ask you how you would handle each of them or if you would allow them at all.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I ask because I want to see where I stand compared to others, especially in relation to its limitations; one detail, no significant pain and its thematic limit.</span> <hr /><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>1. Abyssal Rift</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Scene : </b>A Nosferatu is ambushed by hunters in an alley street and uses waking nightmare in hope to reverse the role.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Illusion : </b>The ground breaks under everyone\u2019s feet, opening a wide bottomless rift black as night.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Question :</b> Which interpretation feels better? A, B, C, or none. If so, how would you handle it?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Interpretation A : </b>The Hunter feels the ground break hunter their feet. Each of them falls to the ground and the world around them slowly feels more distant as they ''fall'' further down the chasm. From the Nosferatu perspective, the hunters are on their backs clawing the air and panicking, leaving the Nosferatu ample opportunity to send them to early retirement.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Interpretation B :</b> Since the illusion is already affecting the ground, it can\u2019t affect other\u2019s \u2018\u2019location\u2019\u2019 relative to the place, nor can it obfuscate the Nosferatu. The hunters can see each other standing on a black rift; the hunters feel their whole sense of balance thrown off*.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Some may fall, some more dextrous than others barely manage to stay on their two feet. They can see the Nosferatu walking above the rift unhindered and they realize they have been messing with the wrong kindred. The battle isn\u2019t completely won for the Nosferatu, but the hunter shouldn\u2019t pose much problem.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">*Having done something similar for fun in a VR, walking on the edge of a building into \u2018\u2019nothing\u2019\u2019 it is truly dissorienting. It was fun to experience what an actual, albeit weak, illusion could be like in real life.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Interpretation C :</b> A mix of both A and B. Hunters are affected by the illusions, they, since they all act in accordance with the illusions, they all see themselves fall. However they can clearly see the nosferatu move unhindered in the pit. After a few seconds, some might realize that the walls of the nearby building aren't getting smaller as they fall. The effect is simillar to B.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Opinion :</b> I\u2019m a bit torn. I honestly don\u2019t know which one I\u2019d prefer. I would generally lean toward A if a player used the power on hunters, or on B if a player was the victim of the power to allow some tests to perceive some clues and allow an \u2018\u2019out\u2019\u2019.</span> <hr /><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>2. Solipsism</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Illusion : </b>The victim appears to be alone ; their senses can\u2019t pick up anyone in the vicinity, they feel the crushing weight of solitude.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Question :</b> Is this a valid use of the power?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Valid :</b> The power affects a single feature in the location. Having people in a location is a feature of the location, thus removing everyone is a feature in itself.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Invalid :</b> This affects too many features, the power can\u2019t change more than a single person, every person in the room is too many.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Opinion : </b>I would allow it, it\u2019s a powerful effect, it kind of overlaps with obfuscate, but both are illusory powers, one just happens to focus on fear more.</span> <hr /><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>3. Sun Rise</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Scene : </b>A Nosferatu is defending its territory from rival kindreds they caught feeding in an alleyway of their territory. They use waking nightmare to conjure the image of a rising sun to urge them to flee.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Illusion : </b>The Sun rises in the distance, its glaring rays begin to reach the kindred in the alleyway.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Question 1:</b> Can Nightmare conjure illusions of the sun? Or fire?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Opinion : </b>On one end, the Sun and fire are definitely among the scariest things for vampires. On the other, it feels a bit weird thematically for a vampire. I\u2019m torn.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Question 2 :</b> If yes, which interpretation sounds like the correct one?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Interpretation A : </b>The kindred affected by the illusion feel the burning gaze of the sun. They test against fear frenzy at -1 (if humanity 5 or above) or at -3 (if humanity 4 or lower). Those that fail run away, seeking the nearest available cover from the sun. Those that succeed realize that while the air feels hotter and the sun is uncomfortable, they don\u2019t actually feel significant pain so they ignore its effect. The most informed kindred realize that they are facing a Nosferatu that can conjure illusions; the less informed ones wonder why they are suddenly resistant to the sun. Let\u2019s hope, for their sakes, that they don't think they can resist the true sun.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Interpretation B : </b>The kindred affected by the illusion feel the burning gaze of the sun. They test against fear frenzy at -1 (if humanity 5 or above) or at -3 (if humanity 4 or lower). Those that fail run away, seeking the nearest available cover from the sun. Those that succeed might realize that there is an illusion, but the sun still feels real, and while it doesn\u2019t hurt <i>significantly </i>it\u2019s still hot and uncomfortable and they can feel their beast panicking as they urge them to flee for cover. They might stave off the frenzy for a bit longer, but they should not linger. They need to test for frenzy at each interval of fake \u2018\u2019sunlight damage\u2019\u2019 they would take.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Opinion : </b>I would go toward B. This is where i would probably end up using Marcus house rule for rolling composure + resolve \u2013 Nightmare to ignore the effect.</span> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Do you have any other interesting examples that come to mind that you allowed or your storyteller allowed you, or a fellow coterie mate, to use in a chronicle?</span></div> </div> </div> </div>Scene A: option B is my sure pick. Being aware that's an hallucination can give you the chance to make a composure roll to try to get up and act but it doesn't get you rid of the feeling same way as when you're into Virtual reality trying to walk a tiny piece of wood upon the void. I personally struggle. this is represented by the malus you get to any action if you are even able to act based on the composure roll (I could accept not to give the malus on a excepcional success on conposure roll).<br /> <br /> Scene B: I would not allow something/someone to disappear. This is what Obfuscate does. Walking Nightmare affects features. You can change how the victim sees ONE person (per activation), not making it disappear. I used once to have the victim sourrouded by monsters even his allies (by multiple activation).<br /> <br /> Scene C: it's surely possible. Same application as before. If you are somehow aware it's an Illusion you still roll for frenzy (with a possible reduced penalty), and still you get the Nightmare malus to your actions. The distraction is still there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","user":"Marcus","date":". 08-04-2026, 05:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-04T19:43:08-07:00","id":1562706,"title":"","content":"In keeping with my position that Waking Nightmare's big mechanical function is manipulating victims of Face of the Beast and The Grand Delusion, and in keeping with the limitations of those powers and Mortal Terror, my reads are as follows:<br /> <br /> 1. You can affect how the rift reacts while it remains in your presence. You cannot directly control how the hunters react to the rift, and though it manifests to all senses that doesn't make them think they're falling (and in fact I would argue making multiple victims perceive each other to be falling runs afoul of the stipulation that the hallucination only change or add one thing).<br /> <br /> They can see that they ground is falling below them and may react accordingly, and you can try to steer their reaction by controlling how the ground appears to crumble, but you aren't going to automatically inflict the Knocked Down Tilt on them just because you showed them something that would make them lose their footing if it was real.<br /> <br /> 2. I'm with Marcus on this one and will add that the semantic argument that &quot;having people in a location is a feature of the location&quot; is very blatantly not the intended use case between the Nosferatu having a different clan Discipline for hiding people, singular people being another upper limit of the power, and the power's application specifically being additive or transformative.<br /> <br /> (This feeds back into the first example: you can make someone see something as looking like it's unstable or rotting or about to explode, but while cases could be made for a hole in the ground as a change to the ground you're not going to use Nightmare's ability to pull fears out of people's heads to convince them something is no longer there.)<br /> <br /> 3. Following the aesthetic restrictions of both Waking Nightmare and Dread Presence, as well as the stipulations of other powers in Nightmare and Obfuscate, it cannot create a pleasant image nor control the victims' response, which I would argue means, in a hypothetical situation where there's a mortal mixed in with the vampire targets, the sensory experience of the sun is going to be tainted with the touch of nightmares in some way that tips off anybody who doesn't immediately panic and go into frenzy as they would for, say, a theatrically altered UV lamp. (The hallucination can't inflict significant pain, most vampires who have been vampires for long enough to risk frenzy from sunlight damage will not need much exposure at all to recognize it's not actually harming them, and as with the rift, you can't make the hallucination trick the targets into thinking they've been altered in some way without other powers. You could use Waking Nightmare in conjunction with Face of the Beast or The Grand Delusion as a setup for hurting them with Mortal Terror, but simply making a light source or altering the ambient or directed light in a location is unlikely to simulate injury at a level the vampire's Beast will <i>specifically</i> care about beyond the scope of surprise.)<br /> <br /> Fire gets a little less trouble on the aesthetic front but still hits on the &quot;you can't make something that actually seems to affect the target on its own&quot; issue with the rift. The function of Nightmare powers before Mortal Terror is to add levers to help steer targets through the use and manipulation of fear, and the Discipline's use of illusions is based on this magical sleight of hand rather than the ability to selectively warp reality in a truer sense than making somebody have a really bad day.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:06:12-07:00","id":1562719,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power?p=1562706#post1562706\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In keeping with my position that Waking Nightmare's big mechanical function is manipulating victims of Face of the Beast and The Grand Delusion, and in keeping with the limitations of those powers and Mortal Terror, my reads are as follows:<br /> <br /> 1. You can affect how the rift reacts while it remains in your presence. You cannot directly control how the hunters react to the rift, and though it manifests to all senses that doesn't make them think they're falling (and in fact I would argue making multiple victims perceive each other to be falling runs afoul of the stipulation that the hallucination only change or add one thing).<br /> <br /> They can see that they ground is falling below them and may react accordingly, and you can try to steer their reaction by controlling how the ground appears to crumble, but you aren't going to automatically inflict the Knocked Down Tilt on them just because you showed them something that would make them lose their footing if it was real.</div> </div> </div> </div>To clarify, by option B I essentially was in line with this interpretation of yours.<br /> <br /> The victims perceive the fall through all their senses, including their sense of balance, which could manifest as vertigo or dizziness. However, they do not literally see themselves falling into the void. Instead, they see the solid ground opening beneath them. They feel the emptiness below, experience the sensation of falling, and suffer the resulting disorientation, without actually seeing themselves or the others physically plunge downward.<br /> <br /> As a result, one person might collapse to the ground screaming, while someone more composed might manage to maintain their balance and continue acting. I still think the VR analogy fits well: even when you know the experience is artificial, some people are more capable than others of overcoming the instinctive reaction and walking across the apparent void.<br /> <br /> Mechanically, I would represent this through a roll. Nightmare is a four-dot power, so I think it deserves a concrete and reliable effect rather than one that is excessively dependent on interpretation or randomness.<br /> <br /> Once the activation roll succeeds, the victim is affected regardless of whether they understand that it is an illusion. I would require both PCs and NPCs to roll Resolve + Composure, with a penalty equal either to the Nightmare rating or to the number of successes scored on the activation roll, in order to act normally.<br /> <br /> Even if they succeed, they would still suffer a penalty equal to the Nightmare rating on their actions due to the distraction (hallucination is still there), unless they achieve an exceptional success on the Resolve + Composure roll.<br /> <br /> However, this is only my homebrew ruling, intended to bring the power in line with other four-dot abilities. It is not part of the core rules.<br /> <br /> Generally we are aligned the way we read the power.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-06T14:43:56-07:00","id":1562794,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power?p=1562706#post1562706\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In keeping with my position that Waking Nightmare's big mechanical function is manipulating victims of Face of the Beast and<br /> The Grand Delusion, ...</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don\u2019t disagree with your overall analysis, I\u2019m just not sure I fully agree with that position. Mostly because once you\u2019ve done grand delusion or face of the beast, any extra illusion is just extra.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">For face of the beast, the target has already seen something that scared them, they are already basically forced to flee the scene.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">For Grand Delusion, it adds an extra layer of \u2018\u2019realness\u2019\u2019 to the illusion, but it\u2019s already powerful enough on its own. The extra vitae spent on waking nightmares after having spent 2 for Grand delusion is a pretty hefty cost.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Since the power can be used on its own, unlike Dominate 4 or Majesty 4, it has to have usefulness on its own.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I find that the true strength of Nigthmare compared to other mental disciplines is that it\u2019s quick to use, it doesn\u2019t require as much set up as the others.<br /> Dominate is fairly quick, but limited in number of targets and Majesty is quite slow to build up and gives others plenty of opportunities to lash out.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As written, I think the power works pretty well on its own, i think it could have benefited from more mechanical guidelines.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Marcus fix is pretty good for a good quick fix. I think having a list of conditions could have been interesting as well.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">On another note, it\u2019s a shame that mortal terror doesn\u2019t really interact with Waking nightmare on its own, it could have been cool.</span><br /> <hr /><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>1. Abyssal rift</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">You both convinced me. I think interpretation B is the most in line with the rule as written.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I would run the power, as Marcus explained : Hunteds would need to roll a dexterity + athletics at \u2013 Nightmare dots to retain their footing, as the illusion would mean they don\u2019t feel the ground at all and throw their sense of balance off.</span> <hr /><b><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">2. Solipsism</span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">This one is definitely a stretch, I agree.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">My initial reason for thinking it could be valid is that the very nature of illusions will involve some obfuscation :</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">An illusory wall of fire in an alley way will cut line of sight.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">A torrential rain of blood will make both hearing and listening difficult.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">A thick fog will cut line of sight.</span></li> </ul><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">It might be too powerful, however, and on that ground alone, I would probably restrict it, since Waking Nightmare has no inherent \u2018\u2019counterplay\u2019\u2019 unlike most mental disciplines.</span> <hr /><b><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">3. Sunrise</span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"> This one I\u2019m still not convinced I like, simply for thematic reasons, but I would probably allow it anyway. This is purely vibe based.</span> <hr /><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Thanks for the insights <span style=\"font-family:Segoe UI Emoji\">\ud83d\ude0a</span></span><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-06T15:15:17-07:00","id":1562798,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power?p=1562794#post1562794\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don\u2019t disagree with your overall analysis, I\u2019m just not sure I fully agree with that position. Mostly because once you\u2019ve done grand delusion or face of the beast, any extra illusion is just extra.</div> </div> </div> </div>My position is that without the extra support of the preceding powers, you have no mechanical backing guaranteed for the target having the reality they engage with altered \u2014 putting a terrifying phantasmal creature in front of the only entrance to your Haven might discourage a would-be intruder, but unless you actually Frighten them or give them a Delusion that feeds into it there's nothing preventing them from gathering their courage and trying to break in anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For face of the beast, the target has already seen something that scared them, they are already basically forced to flee the scene. </div> </div> </div>Face of the Beast gives the Frightened Condition for a thing you know the target is afraid of (or, with Dread Presence, yourself). The thing they're afraid of is not necessarily in the scene and Waking Nightmare lets you introduce their fear into the scene even if it would be implausible (or beyond the scope of Dread Presence to fabricate).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For Grand Delusion, it adds an extra layer of \u2018\u2019realness\u2019\u2019 to the illusion, but it\u2019s already powerful enough on its own. The extra vitae spent on waking nightmares after having spent 2 for Grand delusion is a pretty hefty cost. </div> </div> </div>As with Face of the Beast, the ability to steer a character's behavior (or force them to spend Willpower to avoid acting in line with the delusion, which you can set up a few nights in advance with the support of Dread Presence) without needing to visibly add or alter the target's surroundings outside of their own head. The limits of the beliefs implanted by The Grand Delusion can be pushed past by altering the subject's reality in a way that forces the issue \u2014 you may not be able to make the target believe that they must attack their friends, but you can make them believe their friends are plotting to harm them and then show them their friends acting in a way that makes them more inclined to attack them in self-defense/rage/fear/etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Since the power can be used on its own, unlike Dominate 4 or Majesty 4, it has to have usefulness on its own. </div> </div> </div>And it largely does this. Even without hard mechanical effects or interaction with the other dots, Waking Nightmare allows you to disguise yourself with an unreal appearance, speak via illusory proxy, create obstacles terrifying enough to at least discourage targets who don't know illusions are at work. Its relative lack of startup time doesn't negate its continuation of the trend where the higher powers of the mental Discipline ladders are more effective with support from the lower tiers.<br /> <br /> Getting a bit wiggly with analogies, Nightmare is the dagger to Obfuscate's cloak \u2014 while the latter allows you to subtract things from the senses of everyone around you, the former lets you add things to the personal reality of individual people in isolation from everyone else's perceptions. Dominate and Majesty are obvious in use to onlookers by the nature of their effects (even if you don't know supernatural powers are at work, you can see the vampire being compelling or giving instructions), but Nightmare doesn't require the character to visibly do anything to change the way its targets interact with the world around them, Waking Nightmare being the most narratively versatile tool in the kit to that end.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-07T09:29:06-07:00","id":1562833,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power?p=1562798#post1562798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My position is that without the extra support of the preceding powers, you have no mechanical backing guaranteed for the target having the reality they engage with altered \u2014 putting a terrifying phantasmal creature in front of the only entrance to your Haven might discourage a would-be intruder, but unless you actually Frighten them or give them a Delusion that feeds into it there's nothing preventing them from gathering their courage and trying to break in anyway.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Ah, I understand then. RAW does support your interpretation; there\u2019s indeed nothing that can mechanically force any reaction from the target.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Humans might be subject to breaking points as per CoD rules of noticing supernatural phenomena, but that\u2019s pretty much all I can think off.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power?p=1562798#post1562798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Face of the Beast gives the Frightened Condition for a thing you know the target is afraid of (or, with Dread Presence, yourself). The thing they're afraid of is not necessarily in the scene and Waking Nightmare lets you introduce their fear into the scene even if it would be implausible (or beyond the scope of Dread Presence to fabricate).</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Seems you\u2019re right. I read the power again and it says nothing about conjuring the fear. I had assumed it would conjure some form of images to summon specific fears, as it seems weird to invoke arachnophobia without any spiders involved, but I\u2019m fine with Face of the Beast conjuring unguided terror.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power?p=1562798#post1562798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> As with Face of the Beast, the ability to steer a character's behavior (or force them to spend Willpower to avoid acting in line with the delusion, which you can set up a few nights in advance with the support of Dread Presence) without needing to visibly add or alter the target's surroundings outside of their own head. The limits of the beliefs implanted by The Grand Delusion can be pushed past by altering the subject's reality in a way that forces the issue \u2014 you may not be able to make the target believe that they must attack their friends, but you can make them believe their friends are plotting to harm them and then show them their friends acting in a way that makes them more inclined to attack them in self-defense/rage/fear/etc.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Oh certainly, it\u2019s a powerful combo. It can be used that way; however, I think the benefit from Waking Nightmare, would be different in that scenario.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As much as I like your example, the power doesn\u2019t actually do anything more mechanically to push the victim toward acting toward their friend. They already believe that their friends are plotting against them; any illusion invoked simply confirms an already held belief. Since there\u2019s no mechanical benefit to the power, the extra vitae spent doesn\u2019t add that much more to the delusion in effect.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">What I think it <i>can </i>do is make the delusion continue past its expiration. If a victim becomes delusional and thinks their friends are plotting against them, after the duration, they will probably feel bad, but since the delusion goes away, they will probably chalk it off to anxiety induced by that creepy-looking guy when they told them their friends were planning to murder them in their sleep.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">However, if they hear their friend sneak into their home at night and hear them discuss how to proceed with the plan, then that creepy guy was right all along.<br /> <br /> The forced delusion goes away, but now, to them, it\u2019s not simply an irrational fear or a belief anymore; it\u2019s something they actually saw.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power?p=1562798#post1562798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> And it largely does this. Even without hard mechanical effects or interaction with the other dots, Waking Nightmare allows you to disguise yourself with an unreal appearance, speak via illusory proxy, create obstacles terrifying enough to at least discourage targets who don't know illusions are at work. Its relative lack of startup time doesn't negate its continuation of the trend where the higher powers of the mental Discipline ladders are more effective with support from the lower tiers.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">It\u2019s definitely useful, and it\u2019s fairly cheap at 1 vitae.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The lack of clear mechanical guidance is somewhat annoying as a storyteller, but nothing insurmountable. If i ever rewrite the power for my home games i\u2019ll probably add a list of possible conditions.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561998-nightmare-4-\u2013-waking-nightmare-\u2013-how-do-you-handle-the-power?p=1562798#post1562798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Getting a bit wiggly with analogies, Nightmare is the dagger to Obfuscate's cloak \u2014 while the latter allows you to subtract things from the senses of everyone around you, the former lets you add things to the personal reality of individual people in isolation from everyone else's perceptions.[... ] Waking Nightmare being the most narratively versatile tool in the kit to that end.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I think that\u2019s an apt analogy.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I wouldn\u2019t say Nightmare is limited to adding only, but it can also modify perception, but that\u2019s semantics, i get what you mean.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As with any narratively focused power, its usefulness is going to vary from storyteller to storyteller.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Thanks for the clarifications <span style=\"font-family:Segoe UI Emoji\">\ud83d\ude0a</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}